{"full":"hf:hugging-apps/kandinsky-5-time-adapter","spaceId":"hugging-apps/kandinsky-5-time-adapter","name":"Kandinsky 5.0 Time Adapter","source":"huggingface","description":"FPS-controlled image-to-video with Kandinsky 5.0","category":"AI & machine learning","language":"Python","topics":["gradio","mcp-server","region:us"],"technologies":["Python"],"likes":3,"created":"2026-08-07T06:23:56.000Z","updated":"2026-08-07T07:51:18.000Z","license":"mit","runtime":"RUNNING","appUrl":"https://hugging-apps-kandinsky-5-time-adapter.hf.space/","demo":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/hugging-apps/kandinsky-5-time-adapter","availability":"available","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-10T20:59:28.371Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-10T20:59:28.371Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-10T20:59:28.371Z","color":"#3c3250","ink":"#e4d4ff","symbol":"⏱️","overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Image-to-video generation with an explicit target FPS control.","kandinskylab/Time-Adapter is a temporal adapter for Kandinsky 5.0 I2V Lite 5s. The FPS condition modulates the DiT's global time embedding, while a per-frame latent time signal modulates the visual tokens — so the same image and prompt can be animated slowly (low target FPS) or quickly (high target FPS). The clip is also written at that frame rate, so a low target FPS stretches the same generated frames over a longer playback.","The demo runs the authors' timeadapter branch of kandinsky-5, vendored in kandinsky/ (the only edit is dropping the package init's re-exports, so that importing a module doesn't pull in the distributed / torch.cuda.setdevice launcher path), with the released TA-only step-5750 checkpoint and the authors' own k5i2vtimeadapterstep5750taonly.yaml config."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/hugging-apps/kandinsky-5-time-adapter/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"a3d5da2b66f11d00c0054ad5328cdac6704580432e10f23ea6012766c1934a0e","extractedAt":"2026-10-10T20:59:28.449Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-10T